{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies small-scale possession as a criminal offense. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine can be offered a path to services instead of prosecution—provided they meet strict conditions. Otherwise, they face misdemeanor charges carrying up to half a year in custody. The law also tightens restrictions on sales near areas like playgrounds and increases enforcement presence in public spaces.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes local flexibility by urging counties to design their own support systems to steer people away from incarceration and toward care. While supporters argue this empowers regions to tailor responses to their capacity, critics warn it may lead to uneven outcomes across jurisdictions. Police are instructed to contact a specialized behavioral health line if someone qualifies for an alternative route. That route requires no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and physical stability.\n\n***\n\nIf a health team cannot arrive within half an hour, officers must proceed with arrest. There’s no option to delay or linger—time constraints are rigid. Chief Bob Day emphasized that law enforcement cannot act as social workers; their role is to enforce the law while facilitating access to help when possible. The system hinges on quick coordination, and delays mean detention.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how to balance public safety with human dignity. In some parts of the country, people over a certain age are learning to navigate new rules while still trying to eat well, shop for essentials, and maintain dignity—even when their 